First, we discuss the chemistry of boron-capped clathrochelates. These complexes are facile to synthesize by metal-templated condensation reactions. The synthesis is modular, and it is straightforward to implement structural variations. Importantly, it is possible to attach functional groups such as amines, pyridines, or carboxylic acids to the ligand periphery. Other noteworthy features of boron-capped clathrochelates are high thermodynamic and kinetic stability, tunable redox potential, and good solubility.
Next, we show that clathrochelate-based metalloligands can be used to build molecularly defined metal-ligand assemblies of nanoscale dimensions. Different molecular architectures are described, including coordination cages with unusual gyrobifastigium or square orthobicupolalike structures. Metalloligands containing multiple clathrochelate complexes are particularly well suited to build large metal-ligand assemblies $(>3{\mathrm{~nm}})$ with minimal synthetic efforts.
Boron-capped clathrochelates have also been investigated in the context of materials chemistry. Linear or cross-linked clathrochelate polymers were found to display permanent porosity. Furthermore, such polymers were used to prepare conducting films on electrodes. Clathrochelate metalloligands are well suited to prepare metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). The high stability of clathrochelates ensures compatibility with harsh reaction conditions, and it mitigates potential problems such as exchange reactions. Boron-capped clathrochelates can be decorated with functional groups in lateral and apical position, and it is possible to use these complexes as multi-connected nodes in polymeric structures.
Overall, we hope to convey the utility of clathrochelate complexes in supramolecular chemistry and materials science. The work published thus far gives a first glimpse of the potential of these compounds, but there are other directions, which wait to be explored. For example, it will be interesting to study the properties of nanostructures based on chiral clathrochelate complexes. Furthermore, the redox and magnetic properties of clathrochelates may give rise to novel functional materials. Given that clathrochelates are straightforward to prepare, we hope that others will join the efforts to explore the supramolecular and materials chemistry of these interesting molecular building blocks.
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Coordinatively saturated complexes can be formed by encapsulation of metal ions within macropolycyclic ligands. Busch suggested the term ‘clathrochelate’ to describe complexes of this kind.1 Alternatively, they are sometimes referred to as ‘cage complexes’.2 If the encapsulated ions are alkali or alkaline earth metal ions, the term ‘cryptate’ is commonly employed.3 Early examples of transition metal clathrochelates were reported by the groups of Rose,4 Holm,5 Goedken,6 and Sargeson7 (Figure 1).
Following these initial reports, clathrochelate complexes have been studied extensively over the last decades. For a comprehensive summary of the chemistry of clathrochelates, we would like to refer to two books published in $2002^{8}$ and in 2017.2 Biological applications of clathrochelates are discussed in a recent review article.9
In the following overview, we will focus in particular on arylboron-capped clathrochelates. A convenient way to prepare these complexes is via a metal-templated condensation reaction of a dioxime and an arylboronic acid (Scheme 1). It is worth noting that alkylboronic acids can be incorporated as well.2,8 However, for the applications described below, arylboronic acids are more relevant. $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions can be used to template these reactions. The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ion is a less efficient template, and the yields tend to be lower compared to what is observed for reactions with $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ salts. The 4d transition metal ruthenium can be incorporated by employing $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ or $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ salts. In the latter case, in situ reduction to $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ is performed before complex formation. 10
Cobalt clathrochelates have been isolated in the $1+$ , the $+2$ , and the $+3$ oxidation state.11,12 In contrast, the chemistry of clathrochelates containing iron and ruthenium is largely limited to the $+2$ oxidation state, even though one can observe a quasi-reversible oxidation to the $+3$ state by electrochemistry. Using a dioxime with electron-withdrawing chloro substituents, the isolation of an $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{I}}$ clathrochelate was recently accomplished.13
## Scheme 1. Synthesis of mononuclear, arylboron-capped clathrochelate complexes
Clathrochelate complexes can be decorated with functional groups in apical position (Scheme  1, R’) by using the corresponding arylboronic acid. Notably, donor groups such as pyridine or carboxylic acids can be employed without compromising the metal-templated reaction. The lateral groups (Scheme  1, R) attached to the clathrochelate can be modified via the dioxime. In several projects, we have used nioxime (a dioxime with a cyclohexyl group). This dioxime is commercially available, and it gives rise to complexes, which tend to be well soluble in organic solvents.
Clathrochelate complexes are often very stable, and arylboron-capped complexes are no exception. The boronate ester groups are not susceptible to hydrolysis, and ligand exchange reactions are only observed under very forcing conditions. Considering the handling of these complexes, it is worth mentioning that $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ clathrochelates are not air and moisture sensitive.
The inert character of boron-capped clathrochelates offers the opportunity to perform postsynthetic modifications. For example, clathrochelates can be used in palladium-catalyzed crosscoupling reactions (Scheme 2, top).14–16 For modifications in lateral position, substitution reactions are an interesting option. It is possible to prepare clathrochelate complexes containing between one and six chloro atoms in lateral position. These complexes are amenable to substitution reactions using N-, O-, and S-nucleophiles (Scheme 2, bottom).2,8 There are other possibilities for the post-synthetic functionalization of clathrochelates, and a comprehensive discussion is provided in ref. 2.
## Scheme 2. Post-synthetic modification of clathrochelate complexes
When metal-templated reactions are performed with phenoldioximes instead of simple 1,2- dioximes, dinuclear clathrochelate complexes are obtained (Scheme 3). The first example of such a complex featuring a methylboron cap was reported by Chaudhuri and co-workers.17 We have subsequently shown that arylboron-capped complexes can be prepared in a similar fashion.18 Suited metal templates are $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ or $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions. The resulting $\mathrm{{M}^{I I}/\mathrm{{M}^{I I}}}$ clathrochelates have an overall charge of minus one. This feature is of importance for the applications described in section three. Dinuclear iron clathrochelates stand out because they are thermodynamically more stable than the corresponding cobalt or zinc complexes.19 Furthermore, they are accessible as anionic $\mathrm{Fe^{II}/F e^{I I}}$ complexes or as neutral $\mathrm{Fe^{II}/F e^{I I I}}$ complexes.
As in the case of mononuclear clathrochelates, it is possible to introduce functional groups such as pyridines,18 carboxylic acids,20 or amines21 in apical position by using the corresponding boronic acid during the synthesis. The post-synthetic introduction of functional groups is also possible via Pd-catalyzed cross-coupling reactions.22,23
## Scheme 3. Synthesis of dinuclear, arylboron-capped clathrochelate complexes
The lateral size of clathrochelates can be varied substantially by changing the groups R attached to the phenoldioxime. Figure 2 shows the cores of two mononuclear and one dinuclear clathrochelate as determined by crystallography (apical aryl groups are removed for clarity). By comparing the space-filing representations of the structures, it is apparent that the clathrochelate with lateral phenyl groups (Figure 2b) is significantly wider than the clathrochelate based on glyoxime (Figure 2a). Even wider complexes can be obtained with dinuclear clathrochelates (Figure 2c). As discussed in the next section, the lateral size can be a decisive parameter for controlling the architecture of supramolecular assemblies. Generally, clathrochelates are well soluble in polar organic solvents due to their three-dimensional shape, which disfavors $\pi$ - stacking interactions. It is possible to tune the solubility by modifying the lateral and apical groups R and R’.
Figure 2. The molecular structures of mono- and dinuclear clathrochelate complexes with hydrogen atoms (a), phenyl groups (b), and $t-$ butyl groups in lateral position (c). The aryl groups attached to the boron atoms are not shown for clarity. White: H, grey: C; blue: N; yellow: B; red: O; dark green: Co, orange: Fe.
## 2. CLATHROCHELATE COMPLEXES IN SUPRAMOLECULAR CHEMISTRY
As described in the last section, boron-capped clathrochelates can be obtained in one-pot reactions from easily accessible starting materials. Furthermore, they are very robust compounds, and it is possible to decorate clathrochelates with a variety functional groups. These features make boron-capped clathrochelates interesting structural elements for the construction of metalloligands.24,25
In the area of coordination-driven supramolecular chemistry, ligands with pyridyl donor groups are of special importance.26–28 Iron clathrochelate complexes with terminal 4-pyridyl groups can be prepared in high yield by reaction of three commercially available compounds, namely nioxime, 4-pyridylboronic acid, and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ .14 The two terminal N-donor atoms are $1.5\mathrm{nm}$ apart from each other (Figure 3, left side). This value is similar to what is found for pyridylfunctionalized porphyrins, which have been used extensively as metalloligands.29,30 The dipyridyl clathrochelate was employed for the construction of heterometallic macrocycles containing iridium31 or rhenium.14 A structurally related ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ clathrochelate was used to bridge two zinc tetraphenylporphyrin complexes.32 In the presence of a sacrificial electron donor, lightinduced reduction of the cobalt complex was observed.
Figure 3. Clathrochelate-based metalloligands with terminal 4-pyridyl groups. The structures of the two shorter ligands are based on X-ray crystallography, whereas the structures of the other ligands were obtained by molecular modeling. The three longest ligands have octyl side chains, which are substituted by methyl groups for the clarity of this figure.
It is straightforward to extend the length of clathrochelate-based dipyridyl ligands. One possibility is to use a mixture of a diboronic acid and 4-pyridylboronic acid during the synthesis.
This kind of statistical reaction will lead to a mixture of products, from which a metalloligand with two clathrochelate cores can be isolated by selective precipitation or chromatography. Pyridyl-terminated metalloligands with lengths of 2.7 and $3.2~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3) were prepared in this fashion. A second possibility to extend the lengths of these ligands is to connect pre-formed clathrochelate complexes with iodo or alkynyl functions via Sonogashira coupling reactions. Using this strategy, ligands with lengths of up to $5.4\mathrm{nm}$ were prepared (Figure 3).14
Pyridyl-terminated clathrochelate ligands were used by Ardavan and co-workers as linkers for heterometallic $\mathrm{Cr}{\mathrm{{n}}{\mathrm{{i}}}}$ rings (Scheme 4).33 These paramagnetic rings with a well-defined $S=\%$ ground state are of interest as molecular spin qubits. By variation of the metalloligand, they were able to change the minimum distance between the paramagnetic rings in the range of $1.9\mathrm{-}3.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , thereby providing control over the inter-qubit quantum interactions.
Scheme 4. A clathrochelate-based dipyridyl ligand as a nanoscale spacer for heterometallic Cr-Ni rings (Cr: dark green balls, Ni: pink balls)
The combination of ditopic ligands with terminal pyridyl groups and $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions is expected to give metal-ligand assemblies of the general formula ${\mathrm{Pd}}_{n}{\mathrm{L}}_{2n}$ . For ligands with a linear geometry and 3-pyridyl groups at the end, assemblies of type $\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ had been reported.34 Larger assemblies of type $\mathrm{Pd}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{12}$ were obtained when clathrochelates with terminal 3-pyridyl groups were employed (Scheme 5a; the graphic of the cage is based on crystallographic data, and only one ligand is shown for clarity).35 Most likely, assemblies with less than six palladium ions are disfavored due to steric interaction between the clathrochelate side chains. This hypothesis was corroborated by the following observation: when the reaction was carried out with a metalloligand containing a ‘thin’ clathrochelate core $\mathrm{(R=H)}$ ), the quantitative formation of a $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ tetrahedron instead of a $\mathrm{Pd}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{12}$ octahedron was observed (Scheme 5b).36 Furthermore, it was found that the thermodynamic stability of $\mathrm{Pd}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{12}$ cages is strongly influenced by the lateral size of the clathrochelate side chains.
Scheme 5. Synthesis of octahedral or tetrahedral palladium coordination cages (Pd: cyan balls)
The results summarized in Scheme 5 suggest that the ligand aspect ratio (length-to-with ratio) of a rod-like ligand can be a decisive element in controlling the geometry of metal-ligand assemblies. Further evidence for the importance of the aspect ratio was obtained by studying the synthesis of FeII-based coordination cages. Reactions of formylpyridyl-terminated clathrochelates with 4-methylaniline and ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ resulted in the formation of two types of cages (Scheme 6). A ‘thin’ clathrochelate with methyl groups in lateral position gave a tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe_{4}L_{6}}$ cage, whereas a ‘thicker’ nioxime-based clathrochelate gave a cubic $\mathrm{Fe}_{8}\mathrm{L}_{12}$ structure (Scheme 6; the graphics of the cages are based on molecular modelling). In a similar fashion, by combining amine-terminated clathrochelates with formylpyridine and ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ , longer ligands where created, which formed tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe_{4}L_{6}}$ cages, independent of the groups in the lateral position.33
Scheme 6. Synthesis of tetrahedral or cubic iron coordination cages (Fe: orange balls)
The importance of the lateral size of the metalloligands was also noted in investigations using tetrapyridyl ligands. These ligands were synthesized by cross-coupling reactions using tetrabrominated clathrochelate complexes and 4-pyridylboronic acid. Subsequently, reactions with cis-blocked $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes were studied. A first unexpected result was the formation of a $\mathrm{Pt}_{8}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ coordination cage with a gyrobifastigium-like geometry (Scheme 7a).15 The rectangular faces of the polyhedron are paneled by the tetratopic metalloligands. Later, we found that by varying the clathrochelate core of the ligand, we could also obtain $\mathrm{Pt_{8}L_{4}}$ assemblies with a tetragonal barrel structure (Scheme 7b), pentagonal $\mathrm{Pt}_{10}\mathrm{L}_{5}$ barrels (Scheme 7c), and a $\mathrm{Pt}_{16}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ cage with an unprecedented square orthobicupola-like structure (Scheme 7d).37,38 The latter assembly has a molecular weight of more than $23\mathrm{\kDa}$ and a diameter of $4.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , making it one of the largest, structurally characterized $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{II}}$ cages in the CCDC data base.
Scheme 7. Synthesis of platinum coordination cages using tetratopic metalloligands (Pt: green balls)
As discussed above, metalloligands with two clathrochelate complexes can be made by using a mixture of a diboronic acid and an arylboronic acid during the synthesis. In some cases, the desired metalloligand can be isolated by selective precipitation.14 A more general method to purify these clathrochelates is size exclusion chromatography. Being able to access long metalloligands with minimal synthetic efforts, we have examined the formation of large (up to $3\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ) Pd2L4-type coordination cages (Scheme 8).28,39 Interestingly, when comparing the thermodynamic stability of cages formed from clathrochelates with different lateral groups, it appeared that the cages from ligands with the sterically more demanding cyclohexl groups are more stable than the cages from ligands with methyl groups. It was proposed that the close packing of the lipophilic side chains contributes to the enhanced stability in polar solvents.
## Scheme 8. Synthesis of $\mathbf{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ -type coordination cages (Pd: cyan balls)
Recently, we have started to explore the supramolecular chemistry of metalloligands containing three clathrochelate complexes. A mixture of 1,3,5-benzenetriboronic acid, 3-pyridylboronic acid, nioxime, and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ gave a tritopic ligand with terminal pyridyl groups in one step. When this ligand was combined with $\mathrm{[Pd(CH_{3}C N)_{4}](B F_{4})_{2}}$ , a $\mathrm{Pd}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ coordination cage was formed (Scheme 9).40 The six $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions occupy the vertices of an octahedron, and the eight metalloligands panel the faces. With a maximum Pd···Pd distance of $3.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , this cage is the largest, structurally characterized ${\mathrm{Pd}}_{6}{\mathbf{L}}_{n}$ cage described so far. The void space of this cage is estimated to be around $2800\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ , as determined with VIODOO software. Consequently, the cage is potentially suited to encapsulate very large guest molecules.
## Scheme 9. Synthesis of a large $\mathbf{Pd_{6}L_{8}}$ coordination cage (Pd: cyan balls)
In the previous section, we have shown that clathrochelate-based metalloligands are useful building blocks for the construction of molecularly defined nanostructures. In the following section, we will focus on polymeric materials containing clathrochelate complexes.
Lee and co-workers have prepared conducting polymer films by electropolymerization of clathrochelate complexes with bithiophenylboron caps on electrode surfaces (Figure 4a).41,42
Polymers containing Fe, Co or Ru were analyzed electrochemically, and they were found to display metal-dependent redox properties. The sequential electropolymerization of clathrochelate monomers containing different metal ions gave bilayer structures.
Amorphous polymer networks containing dinuclear iron clathrochelates were prepared by Pd-catalyzed crosslinking of a pentabrominated complex with 1,4-benzenediboronic acid (Figure 4b).19 The polymers displayed good thermal and chemical stability. Nitrogen sorption measurements at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ revealed an apparent Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $S A_{\mathrm{BET}}=510\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{\bfg}^{-1}$ . Porous polymers containing mononuclear clathrochelates were made by $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ -templated polycondensation of 1,4-benzenediboronic acid with different dioximes.43 A polymer based on diphenylglyoxime (Figure 4c) gave a surface area of $S A_{\mathrm{BET}}=412\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Metalloligands are also of interest for the preparation of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and coordination polymers.44 Given that polycarboxylates are particularly well suited to prepare MOFs, we were interested to explore mono- and dinuclear clathrochelate complexes decorated with carboxylic acid functions. Clathrochelates with up to five $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ groups in the ligand periphery can be synthesized in one step from readily available starting materials.20 The resulting metalloligands can be used for the preparation of MOFs, and two representative examples are depicted in Scheme 10 and Scheme 11. The 2-dimensional MOF (Scheme 10) contains fourconnected $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ paddlewheel nodes, whereas the 3-dimensional MOF (Scheme 11) features $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ clusters as links.20
It is interesting to note that the dinuclear metalloligands in the 3-dimensional MOF have a geometry which deviates substantially from the standard (pseudo)- $C_{3}$ symmetry (Scheme 1, inset). This flexibility is to be taken into account when designing supramolecular structures from these compounds.
Scheme 10. Synthesis of a 2-dimensional MOF using a mononuclear clathrochelate with carboxylic acid groups in apical position
Scheme 11. Synthesis of a 3-dimensional MOF using a dinuclear clathrochelate with carboxylic acid functions in lateral position.
Dinuclear clathrochelate complexes containing two $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions have a unique property: they show an overall charge of minus one. It is thus possible to prepare negatively charged polypyridyl ligands.18,23 In the context of MOF chemistry, this feature is of interest, because clathrochelatebased pyridyl ligands can be used to compensate the positive charge of the metal nodes. For example, we have prepared a MOF by reaction of a pyridylboron-capped clathrochelate with $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}$ (Scheme 12).18 The metalloligands are connected through octahedral ${\mathrm{Cd}}({\mathrm{DMF}})_{2}$ units, resulting in a 2-dimensional network structure. The network is charge-neutral and anions are absent. After solvent removal, the MOF shows an apparent surface area of $S A_{\mathrm{BET}}=700\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Scheme 12. Synthesis of a 2-dimensional MOF using a dinuclear clathrochelate with pyridyl groups in apical position (Cd: purple balls)
Boron-capped clathrochelate complexes typically display (pseudo)- $C_{3}$ symmetry (Figure 2). By placing functional groups in apical and lateral position, it is possible to make metalloligands with an unusual trigonal bipyramidal geometry. For example, we have prepared a metalloligand with five divergent cyano groups (Scheme 13; the dashed lines highlight the trigonal bipyramidal geometry). The combination of this ligand with $\mathrm{\bfAgClO_{4}}$ resulted in the formation of a 3-dimensional network structure.22 A topological analysis revealed that the structure has a new network topology with the point symbol $(8{\cdot}10^{2})(8^{2}{\cdot}10^{4})(8^{2}{\cdot}10)(8^{3}{\cdot}10^{3})$ . In addition, the net shows self-catenation with a very high topological density of $\mathrm{TD}_{10}=3127$ .
Scheme 13. A dinuclear clathrochelate with five cyano groups arranged in a trigonal bipyramidal fashion
## 4. CONCLUSIONS AND PERSPECTIVES
7) Clathrochelate metalloligands of nanoscale dimensions are readily accessible.
From a synthetic chemistry point of view, the ease of synthesis of clathrochelate metalloligands is one of the most attractive features. For example, let’s consider the tritopic pyridyl ligand depicted in Scheme 9. The metalloligand is prepared in a one-pot synthesis from readily accessible starting materials, and the N-donor atoms are approximately $2.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ apart from each other. By using a different triboronic acid, this distance can be extended to $2.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ .40 It would be less straightforward to synthesize tritopic pyridyl ligands of comparable size and solubility using standard organic coupling chemistry.
Since it is possible to access large ligands containing multiple clathrochelate cores with reasonable synthetic efforts, one can now use these ligands to prepare novel metal-ligand assemblies. In our group, we have focused so far on coordination cages based on di- and tritopic pyridyl ligands (Scheme 8 and 9). Possible next steps are the utilization of ligands with different donor groups (e.g. carboxylates), the preparation of ligands with new geometries (e.g. tetratopic ligands), and the extension from molecularly defined structures to polymers (e.g. MOFs).
From an applications point of view, the physical properties of clathrochelates are of special relevance. The complexes are very stable, and chemical or physical degradation is only observed under forcing conditions. Some clathrochelate complexes are accessible in different oxidation states, and the incorporation of such complexes in supramolecular assemblies or polymers could give redox-switchable materials. The utilization of clathrochelates with redox non-innocent ligands, for example, appears to be an avenue worth exploring. The magnetic properties of some clathrochelates (e.g. CoII complexes) also provide an opportunity to prepare functional supramolecular structures. Finally, we would like to point out that it should be possible to prepare chiral clathrochelates from optically active dioximes. These complexes could be used to prepare porous polymers, with possible applications in chiral separations.
